{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-fd42fed6-ecc7-4b0a-9953-f215249ab356","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-bf6684392e891370","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9018-zpeywim0/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9018-E001.md","content":"# Confirm metering data consumption contract for chargeback reporting\n\n## Description\n\nThis investigation resolves the strategy's flagged open question: the per-project GPU-hour metering data schema is owned by the in-flight GPU usage metering strategy and is not final until that strategy lands. This epic establishes the consumption contract that administrator-facing chargeback reporting builds against — the final (or firmly drafted) data schema, the access interface through which reporting reads the data, and the availability timing — and decides the reporting data-access approach. It produces no reporting code; its deliverable is a written, agreed contract plus a recommendation that downstream reporting work can build against without re-analysis.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Engage with the GPU usage metering workstream to obtain the current draft and track the final per-project GPU-hour metering data schema, including field-level semantics (what counts as a GPU-hour, project attribution, time bucketing).\n- Identify and confirm the access interface through which reporting consumes the metering data (e.g., query API, metrics endpoint, stored dataset — as defined by the metering strategy), and any constraints it imposes on time-range queries.\n- Confirm availability timing of consumable metering data and record the external timing risk.\n- Decide and document the reporting data-access approach, including a fallback approach if the final schema lands late or in a materially different shape.\n- Document the agreed consumption contract (schema, access interface, semantics, timing) as the input to the reporting implementation.\n- Do NOT re-implement or alter metering collection — collection is explicitly out of scope for this strategy.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- The metering data schema is documented at field level, including the semantics of per-project GPU-hour attribution and time bucketing, and is confirmed with the metering workstream owners.\n- The access interface for consuming the metering data is identified and confirmed as usable for selectable time-range reporting queries.\n- A written consumption contract is produced covering schema, access interface, semantics, and availability timing, sufficient for the reporting implementation to proceed without further schema discovery.\n- A fallback data-access approach is documented for the case where the final schema lands late or differs from the draft.\n- If more than one schema/interface outcome remains possible at closure, each bounded outcome is documented together with the reporting approach it implies.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- Resolves the strategy's open question gating HLR-1 (and transitively HLR-2, HLR-3, HLR-4) — the metering data consumption contract that all reporting work in this strategy builds against.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":39483,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-87585dba9a11245f","agent_id":"abc56259f823db85b","session_id":"ca8c4d42-2d41-4182-b458-e5ffdb2fceb6","uuid":"9b20335f-2620-43fa-8a26-c4a0c4227d9a","timestamp":"2026-10-26T21:35:38.197Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-9018"}
