{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-af5b30633e3508fa","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9018\n3\tepic_count: 6\n4\tcritical_path_length: 4\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9018 (GPU chargeback reporting for platform administrators)\n11\t\n12\t## Epic List\n13\t\n14\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n15\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n16\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E001 | Confirm metering data consumption contract for chargeback reporting | Investigation | RHOAI Dashboard | P0 |\n17\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E002 | Administrator GPU-hour consumption reports over selectable time ranges | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P0 |\n18\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E003 | Report export as CSV and stable API for finance tooling | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n19\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E004 | Per-project Kueue quota attribution contract for chargeback views | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n20\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E005 | Admin view of current-month consumption against Kueue quota | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n21\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E006 | Documentation for report semantics and export integration | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n22\t\n23\t## Dependency DAG\n24\t\n25\t```mermaid\n26\tgraph TD\n27\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-9018-E001 metering consumption contract] --> E002[RHAISTRAT-9018-E002 consumption reports]\n28\t    E002 --> E003[RHAISTRAT-9018-E003 CSV export + API]\n29\t    E002 --> E005[RHAISTRAT-9018-E005 consumption vs quota view]\n30\t    E004[RHAISTRAT-9018-E004 quota attribution] --> E005\n31\t    E002 --> E006[RHAISTRAT-9018-E006 documentation]\n32\t    E003 --> E006\n33\t    E004 --> E006\n34\t    E005 --> E006\n35\t```\n36\t\n37\t## DAG Justification\n38\t\n39\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n40\t|------|------|-----------|\n41\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | The metering data schema is owned by the in-flight metering strategy (RHAISTRAT-9990) and is not final; the strategy flags this as an open question. The resolved consumption contract (schema, access interface, availability) determines the reporting data-access approach, so it is a true gate (`rewrite` if the schema shape differs from the assumption). |\n42\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 12 (framework → consumer) | The export and finance API surface exposes the report computation built by the reporting epic; it builds against that report surface and cannot be finalized before it. |\n43\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 12 (framework → consumer) | The quota-comparison view renders the per-project consumption figures computed by the reporting epic. |\n44\t| E004 → E005 | Rule 13 (contract produced, another epic builds against it) | The quota-comparison view builds against the per-project quota attribution contract validated by the Distributed Workloads team. |\n45\t| E002, E003, E004, E005 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation describes report semantics and export integration of what was built, so it is blocked by every Implementation epic in the strategy. |\n46\t\n47\tNotes on deliberate non-edges:\n48\t\n49\t- E004 has no edge to or from E001/E002: quota attribution is derived from Kueue quota objects and is independent of the metering data schema; the epics run in parallel (Tier 1 work).\n50\t- E003 and E005 are parallel: the export/API surface and the quota-comparison view extend different surfaces of the reporting capability and do not build against each other.\n51\t- E006 is not gated on E001 (an Investigation, not an Implementation) and transitively follows all Implementation epics anyway.\n52\t\n53\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n54\t\n55\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n56\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n57\t| HLR-1 | P0 | E002 | Per-project GPU-hour consumption reports over selectable time ranges, computed from metering data. |\n58\t| HLR-2 | P1 | E003 | CSV export + stable API for finance tooling. |\n59\t| HLR-3 | P1 | E005 (supported by E004) | Current-month consumption vs Kueue quota in the Dashboard admin view. E004 (Distributed Workloads) supplies the validated quota attribution the view builds against. |\n60\t| HLR-4 | P2 | E006 | Documentation of report semantics and export integration. Docs epic priority is P1 per the docs-authoring exception (strategy frontmatter `priority: Major` → P1), not P2 from the HLR label. |\n61\t\n62\tHLR-3 split rationale (recorded at the row): HLR-3 is kept out of the P0 reporting epic (E002) although both render in the administrator surface, because the quota-comparison view depends on cross-team quota attribution work (E004, Distributed Workloads, P1). Bundling HLR-3 into E002 would serialize the P0 reporting epic behind another team's P1 validation work and drag that validation to P0 by priority inheritance. The quota comparison is separately schedulable — it extends the reporting surface with quota context and ships independent user value once both inputs exist.\n63\t\n64\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n65\t\n66\t## Health Warnings\n67\t\n68\t1. **Missing strategy sections (non-blocking):** the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria, and no Non-Functional Requirements section; Affected Components is a bullet list without per-component change descriptions or owners. Team ownership was inferred from architecture context (Dashboard → RHOAI Dashboard; Distributed Workloads → Distributed Workloads), and epic acceptance criteria were derived from the HLRs. Human verification of these inferences is recommended.\n69\t2. **External timing risk (genuine unknown, gated):** the metering data schema is owned by RHAISTRAT-9990 (in flight) and finalizes only when that strategy lands. E001 tracks and gates this; all reporting implementation work is Tier 2 (see Tiered Delivery). E002's `gate_failure_impact` is `rewrite` — a materially different schema or access interface changes the reporting data-access approach.\n70\t3. **Scope trap / ungrounded API surface:** HLR-2 requires a \"stable API for finance tooling\" but the strategy does not state which component serves it. The Dashboard architecture context (`dashboard.md`) states the Dashboard's interfaces are Kubernetes-API-only with no private component APIs, so serving a new external-facing report API from the Dashboard is a new interface surface the context cannot back. Flagged for E003 (scored in its signals); the implementing team must decide API placement — this is an implementation decision, not decomposed further.\n71\t4. **Docs epic priority exception:** HLR-4 is P2, but E006 is P1 because docs-authoring epics derive priority from the strategy frontmatter (`priority: Major` → P1). Recorded here so the P2 HLR label vs P1 epic priority is not read as an inconsistency.\n72\t5. **Passive component handling:** Distributed Workloads requires no code change for this strategy (the Dashboard reads Kueue quota CRs via the Kubernetes API per `dashboard.md`); its role is quota-data provider and semantics owner. Because a different team validates it, the passive-component exception applies: E004 is a separate validation Implementation epic owned by the Distributed Workloads team rather than an acceptance criterion on a Dashboard epic.\n73\t\n74\t## Tiered Delivery\n75\t\n76\tThe external dependency (metering data from RHAISTRAT-9990, uncertain timing) admits partial value:\n77\t\n78\t- **Tier 1 — independent of metering landing (start immediately):**\n79\t  - E001 — resolve/track the metering consumption contract with the metering workstream (risk reduction; unblocks Tier 2 the moment the schema firms up).\n80\t  - E004 — per-project Kueue quota attribution contract (fully independent of metering data; the quota side of the comparison view).\n81\t- **Tier 2 — gated on consumable metering data:**\n82\t  - E002, E003, E005 — all reporting, export/API, and comparison work that computes from metering data.\n83\t  - E006 — documentation of what was built (blocked by all Implementations per Rule 11).\n84\t\n85\tIf RHAISTRAT-9990 lands late, Tier 1 still delivers the validated quota attribution and a pinned consumption contract, so Tier 2 implementation starts without re-analysis.\n86\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8f0901c2f864f29c","agent_id":"a53ae90e14f3a23a4","session_id":"ca8c4d42-2d41-4182-b458-e5ffdb2fceb6","uuid":"386e4d3c-0647-4739-b144-984bc0af7da8","timestamp":"2026-10-26T21:38:34.802Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-af5b30633e3508fa","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9018 decomposition"}
