{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ad7121933dc0d766","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9018-E001\n3\ttitle: Confirm metering data consumption contract for chargeback reporting\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9018\n5\tcomponent: Dashboard\n6\tteam: RHOAI Dashboard\n7\ttype: Investigation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies: []\n10\tinvestigation_signals:\n11\t  question_specificity: 1\n12\t  source_accessibility: 0\n13\t  local_runnability: 0\n14\t  cluster_hardware_dependence: -1\n15\t  human_judgment_required: -2\n16\timplementation_type: null\n17\tai_signals: null\n18\tai_implementability: Low\n19\tai_implementability_score: -2\n20\tjira_key: null\n21\tbranch: null\n22\tgated_by: null\n23\tgate_failure_impact: null\n24\t---\n25\t# Confirm metering data consumption contract for chargeback reporting\n26\t\n27\t## Description\n28\t\n29\tThis investigation resolves the strategy's flagged open question: the per-project GPU-hour metering data schema is owned by the in-flight GPU usage metering strategy and is not final until that strategy lands. This epic establishes the consumption contract that administrator-facing chargeback reporting builds against — the final (or firmly drafted) data schema, the access interface through which reporting reads the data, and the availability timing — and decides the reporting data-access approach. It produces no reporting code; its deliverable is a written, agreed contract plus a recommendation that downstream reporting work can build against without re-analysis.\n30\t\n31\t## Scope\n32\t\n33\t- Engage with the GPU usage metering workstream to obtain the current draft and track the final per-project GPU-hour metering data schema, including field-level semantics (what counts as a GPU-hour, project attribution, time bucketing).\n34\t- Identify and confirm the access interface through which reporting consumes the metering data (e.g., query API, metrics endpoint, stored dataset — as defined by the metering strategy), and any constraints it imposes on time-range queries.\n35\t- Confirm availability timing of consumable metering data and record the external timing risk.\n36\t- Decide and document the reporting data-access approach, including a fallback approach if the final schema lands late or in a materially different shape.\n37\t- Document the agreed consumption contract (schema, access interface, semantics, timing) as the input to the reporting implementation.\n38\t- Do NOT re-implement or alter metering collection — collection is explicitly out of scope for this strategy.\n39\t\n40\t## Acceptance Criteria\n41\t\n42\t- The metering data schema is documented at field level, including the semantics of per-project GPU-hour attribution and time bucketing, and is confirmed with the metering workstream owners.\n43\t- The access interface for consuming the metering data is identified and confirmed as usable for selectable time-range reporting queries.\n44\t- A written consumption contract is produced covering schema, access interface, semantics, and availability timing, sufficient for the reporting implementation to proceed without further schema discovery.\n45\t- A fallback data-access approach is documented for the case where the final schema lands late or differs from the draft.\n46\t- If more than one schema/interface outcome remains possible at closure, each bounded outcome is documented together with the reporting approach it implies.\n47\t\n48\t## HLR Traceability\n49\t\n50\t- Resolves the strategy's open question gating HLR-1 (and transitively HLR-2, HLR-3, HLR-4) — the metering data consumption contract that all reporting work in this strategy builds against.\n51\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8f0901c2f864f29c","agent_id":"a53ae90e14f3a23a4","session_id":"ca8c4d42-2d41-4182-b458-e5ffdb2fceb6","uuid":"bac33943-1a3b-4728-8bef-da5ae100b54e","timestamp":"2026-10-26T21:38:38.160Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a